# This repository's own compose graph. One row per `(parent, child)` pair of templates, with how
# many times the parent splices the child.
#
# Beside `rank/graph_edges.sqlc`, on purpose
#   That relation is the edge list of the graphs the filings make; this is the edge list of the
#   graph this repository's queries make. The shape is the same, and so is the claim behind it:
#   both are named, closed expressions where substitution is the only operation. Two edge lists in
#   one directory make that claim something a query can check.
#
# Where the two graphs rule opposite ways
#   `splices` is where the two graphs rule opposite ways on one fact. A parent composing a child
#   twice is ordinary: a query reads a relation the same way however often it is named, so the
#   repeated relation is read once. The same shape in `pm.part` is `checks/jagged_layer`, a
#   violation, because a supply is counted once and one total closes over both occurrences. That
#   difference is what this model adds to `sql-composer` and to BPMN 2.0.
#   `queries/observations/19-duplication.sqlc` sets the two side by side, and it is a query because
#   both sides are relations.
#
# Read from the files, and outside `pm`
#   That line is worth knowing. Everything in `pm` comes from a filed document; this comes from
#   `assets/sqlc/`. So a relation may read it to say something about this repository, and must
#   never join it to a filing to say something about a subject. It is the same line
#   `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` crosses to read `pg_constraint`.
#
# The join is the point, not the scan
#   `examples/shared/tree/mod.rs` is the one scanner, shared by the examples that read the tree.
#   What a `BTreeMap` in a Rust program cannot do is meet a relation, so any law that bounds one
#   side of the tree by the other needs the graph here rather than there.
#
# `inner_joins` is the syntax
#   Who composes whom does not say how, and the how decides whether a bound survives: driving a
#   query from a relation or left-joining it asks *what is missing from the whole*, inner-joining it
#   asks *does this one pair exist*, and the second hands the whole composed relation to every
#   consumer downstream. It is in the SQL text, so the program that reads the text classifies it,
#   and the fact lands here rather than in a second scanner.
#
# `assets/dag/edges.sql` can go stale
#   That is the cost of generating the file and then loading it. `examples/soundness` works the
#   pairs out again through the same module and compares, so a `:compose` added without rerunning
#   the program that writes the file fails, rather than loading an older tree. That is a check for
#   staleness, not two independent routes: it shares the scanner, so it catches the file drifting
#   and could not catch the scanner being wrong.
#
# Probe
#   Run a parent that composes one child twice under `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`: the repeated
#   relation must be served from shared hits, with no reads.
-- public.compose_edge, generated by examples/compositions/main.rs from assets/sqlc/.
SELECT e.parent, e.child, e.splices, e.inner_joins
FROM public.compose_edge e
